{"task_id": "Mbpp/119", "solution": "from typing import Sequence\n\ndef search(arr: Sequence[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Finds the element that appears only once in a sorted array where all other elements appear exactly twice.\"\"\"\n    lo, hi = 0, len(arr) - 1\n\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        # Ensure mid is at an even index to always compare the first element of a potential pair.\n        # If mid is odd, decrement it to make it even.\n        if mid % 2 == 1:\n            mid -= 1\n\n        # If arr[mid] is equal to arr[mid + 1], it means arr[mid] is part of a pair.\n        # The unique element must be in the right half, after this pair.\n        if arr[mid] == arr[mid + 1]:\n            lo = mid + 2\n        # If arr[mid] is different from arr[mid + 1], it means arr[mid]\n        # is either the unique element or the first element of a pair where the unique element\n        # is in the left half (including mid).\n        else:\n            hi = mid\n\n    # When the loop terminates, lo == hi, and arr[lo] is the unique element.\n    return arr[lo]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/109", "solution": "def odd_Equivalent(s: str, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the number of times a binary string, when rotated from 0 to k-1 times, results in an odd number.\n    The string is considered a binary number, and an odd number has its least significant bit (LSB) as '1'.\n    A right rotation moves the last character to the front. The LSB is the character at index `len(s)-1`.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    \n    # If the string is empty, it cannot represent a number, but the problem constraints usually imply non-empty.\n    # Given \"DO NOT add isinstance() checks or raise exceptions for edge cases\", we assume n > 0.\n    \n    ones_in_string = s.count('1')\n\n    # Calculate full cycles of rotations\n    full_cycles = k // n\n    \n    # Calculate remaining rotations after full cycles\n    rem_rotations = k % n\n\n    # Initialize total count from full cycles\n    total_odd_count = full_cycles * ones_in_string\n\n    # Check remaining rotations\n    for i in range(rem_rotations):\n        # Calculate the index in the ORIGINAL string that will be the LSB\n        # after 'i' right rotations.\n        # If original index is 'j', after 'i' right rotations, it moves to (j + i) % n.\n        # We need the character that ends up at the LSB position (n-1).\n        # So, (original_idx + i) % n = n - 1\n        # original_idx = (n - 1 - i) % n\n        lsb_original_idx = (n - 1 - i) % n\n        \n        if s[lsb_original_idx] == '1':\n            total_odd_count += 1\n\n    return total_odd_count"}
