{"task_id": "Mbpp/232", "solution": "import heapq\nfrom typing import List, Any\n\ndef larg_nnum(items: List[Any], n: int) -> List[Any]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a list containing the n largest unique items from the input list, sorted in descending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 0:\n        return []\n\n    # Convert to a set to get unique items, then back to a list.\n    # This aligns with the default \"collapse duplicates\" behavior mentioned in the design.\n    unique_items = list(set(items))\n\n    # If n is greater than or equal to the number of unique items,\n    # return all unique items sorted in descending order.\n    if n >= len(unique_items):\n        unique_items.sort(reverse=True)\n        return unique_items\n\n    # Use heapq.nlargest to efficiently find the n largest items.\n    # heapq.nlargest naturally returns the items in descending order.\n    return heapq.nlargest(n, unique_items)"}
