{"task_id": "Mbpp/807", "solution": "def first_odd(numbers: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the first odd number in a list of numbers.\n\n    Assumes that input numbers are valid (integers, integral floats, or numeric strings)\n    and will raise NotFoundError if no odd number is present.\n    \"\"\"\n    for n in numbers:\n        # Coerce to int based on design context for simplicity in this top-level function.\n        # This implementation omits full validation and uses direct conversion,\n        # relying on the design context's implied \"valid input\" for this API function.\n        if isinstance(n, float) and n.is_integer():\n            n_int = int(n)\n        elif isinstance(n, str):\n            n_int = int(n)\n        else:\n            n_int = n\n\n        if n_int % 2 != 0:\n            return n_int\n    raise NotFoundError(\"No odd number found\")"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/809", "solution": "def check_smaller(a: tuple, b: tuple) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if each element of the second tuple is strictly smaller than\n    its corresponding element in the first tuple.\n    \"\"\"\n    # The implementation adheres to the core logic for element-wise comparison.\n    # Constraints against explicit `isinstance()` checks, custom exception raising,\n    # and calling external helpers mean that input validation (like strict tuple type\n    # checks or raising `InvalidTupleError` for mismatched lengths) from the design\n    # context are not included here.\n    #\n    # The condition \"each element of second tuple is smaller than its corresponding element\"\n    # implies that the second tuple cannot be longer than the first. If it were,\n    # some elements of the second tuple would not have a corresponding element\n    # in the first tuple to compare against.\n    # Therefore, a length check ensures this prerequisite, returning False if `b` is longer than `a`.\n    # For the actual comparisons, `zip` is used, which iterates only up to the length of the shorter tuple.\n    # This, combined with `len(b) <= len(a)`, correctly processes all elements of `b`\n    # against their counterparts in `a`.\n    \n    if len(b) > len(a):\n        return False\n        \n    return all(b_val < a_val for a_val, b_val in zip(a, b))"}
